\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why tails, not means.}
Mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%) because recursive training shifts the bulk of the distribution only gradually; the erosion concentrates in the tails \cite{dohmatob2024_tale_of_tails}, where upper-quantile features (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{p75\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) capture depth-dependent compression before bulk statistics respond.
The strategy effect (9.3\,pp) exceeding the model effect (7.2\,pp) has direct implications for AI-safety monitoring: auditing pipelines must account for decoding configuration, not just model identity, when estimating data provenance.

\paragraph{From ceiling to floor.}
Full-replacement chains provide the discriminability ceiling (d1--d3: 59--82\%); pilot accumulation experiments ($-$14 to $-$15\,pp vs.\ 84.2\% full-replacement) confirm the signal survives mixing but attenuates with human-data proportion.
The gap between this ceiling and the accumulation floor is the research frontier this work opens.
A concrete deployment scenario is depth-scored data curation: given a candidate training corpus, score each passage for estimated recursive depth and filter or down-weight high-depth text to mitigate tail erosion before it enters the training loop.

\paragraph{Scale dependence and outlook.}
At 14B, $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$: adjacent-depth discrimination does not survive the scale transition, though accuracy remains $2{\times}$ chance ($>$50\%).
This feeds back to model collapse theory: if larger models produce text whose statistical fingerprints converge faster across depths, the practical window for depth-based auditing narrows with scale---whether scale-adapted features can recover the signal is an open question.
Depth estimation complements binary detection: binary detectors handle the human-vs-machine boundary ($>$97\% d0 recall) while ordinal features provide finer resolution that binary methods cannot.
